"""Public-domain translated-prose passages (Project Gutenberg, US public domain), marker-annotated.

Text is verbatim from the cited translation: line wraps joined with single spaces, italics underscores and footnote
markers removed. Annotation: pass A (this file) plus an independent model pass B, adjudicated in
data/gold/ner_romanized/public_domain_adjudication.md. Marker grammar as in build_ner_romanized_gold.py.
"""

PASSAGES = [
    dict(doc_id='pd_zh01', background='zh', work='San Kuo, or Romance of the Three Kingdoms (Brewitt-Taylor translation, 1925)', gutenberg_id=77416,
         marked='That [[Tsʻao Tsʻao|p|ord]] had subdued [[Shantung|l|ord]] has been stated before. He memorialised the throne and was rewarded with the title of General with the epithet “Firm Virtue” and a marquisate. At this time the rebellious [[Li Tsʻui|p|ord]] had made himself Ta-ssŭ-ma, or Minister of War, and his colleague styled himself Grand Commander. Their conduct was abominable but no one dared to criticise them. The [[Tai-yü Yang Piao|p|tit]] and the Minister of Agriculture, [[Chu Chien|p|ord]], privately talked with the [[Emperor Hsien|p|tit]] and said, “[[Tsʻao Tsʻao|p|si]] has twenty legions of soldiers and many capable advisers and leaders; it would be well for the Empire if he would lend his support to the imperial family and help to rid the government of this evil party.”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_zh02', background='zh', work='San Kuo, or Romance of the Three Kingdoms (Brewitt-Taylor translation, 1925)', gutenberg_id=77416,
         marked='Then a forged edict went forth making [[Sun Chien|p|ord]] Prefect of [[Changsha|l|ord]], with orders to suppress the rebellion, and in less than two months he reported the district all tranquil. For this he was created Marquis of [[Wuchʻêng|l|ord]]. Further [[Liu Yü|p|ord]] was made magistrate of [[Yuchow|l|ord]] to move against [[Yüyang|l|ord]] and suppress [[Chang Chü|p|ord]] and [[Chang Shun|p|ord]]. The Prefect of [[Taichou|l|ord]] recommended [[Yüan-tê|p|ord]] to [[Liu Yü|p|ord]], who welcomed him and gave him rank and sent him against the rebels. He fought with and worsted them, and entirely broke their spirit. [[Chang Shun|p|ord]] was cruel and his men turned against him. One of his officers then slew him and brought in his head, after which the others submitted. The other leader [[Chang Chü|p|ord]] saw that all was lost and committed suicide.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_zh03', background='zh', work='San Kuo, or Romance of the Three Kingdoms (Brewitt-Taylor translation, 1925)', gutenberg_id=77416,
         marked='In the summer of the year A.D. 189 the [[Emperor|t|sh]] became seriously ill and summoned [[Ho Chin|p|ord]] into the palace to arrange for the future. This man [[Ho|p|ali]] had sprung from a humble family of butchers, but a sister had become a concubine of rank and borne a son to the [[Emperor|t|sh]], named [[Pien|p|ord]]. After this she became Huang-hou or Empress and [[Ho Chin|p|ord]] became powerful. The [[Emperor|t|sh]] had also greatly loved a beautiful girl named [[Wang|p|ord]] who had borne him a son named [[Hsieh|p|ord]]. The [[Empress Ho|p|tit]] had poisoned the girl from jealousy, and the babe had been given into the care of the [[Empress Dowager Tung|p|tit]], who was the mother of the [[Emperor Ling|p|tit]]. She was the wife of [[Liu Chang|p|ord]], the Marquis of [[Tu-ting|l|ord]]. As time went on and the [[Emperor Huan|p|tit]] had no son of his own he adopted the son of the [[marquis|t|ali]], who succeeded as the [[Emperor Ling|p|tit]]. After his accession he had taken his own mother into the palace to live and had conferred upon her the title of Tʻai-hou, or Empress Dowager.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_zh04', background='zh', work='San Kuo, or Romance of the Three Kingdoms (Brewitt-Taylor translation, 1925)', gutenberg_id=77416,
         marked='This [[Kʻung|p|ord]] was a native of [[Chʻüfou|l|ord]] in the old state of [[Lü|l|ord]], one of the twentieth generation in descent from the great [[Teacher Confucius|p|tit]]. He had been noted as a very intelligent lad, somewhat precocious. When ten years old he had gone to see [[Li Ying|p|ord]], the [[Governor|t|ali]], but the doorkeeper demurred to letting him in. But when he said, “I am [[Minister Li|p|tit]]’s intimate friend,” he was admitted. [[Li|p|ali]] asked him what relations had existed between their families that might justify the term intimate. The boy replied, “Of old my ancestor ([[Kʻung|p|ord]]) questioned yours ([[Lao Tzŭ|p|ord]], whose name was [[Li Erh|p|ali]]) concerning ceremonies. So our families have known each other for many generations.” His host was astonished at the boy’s ready wit.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_zh05', background='zh', work='San Kuo, or Romance of the Three Kingdoms (Brewitt-Taylor translation, 1925)', gutenberg_id=77416,
         marked='With this answer [[Kao Shun|p|ord]] returned to [[Lü Pu|p|ord]], who could not decide what to do. Then came a letter from [[Yüan Shu|p|ord]] saying that although [[Kao Shun|p|ord]] had gone to attack [[Liu Pei|p|ord]], yet [[Pei|p|ali]] had not been destroyed and no reward could be given till he was actually taken. [[Lü Pu|p|ord]] railed at what he called the breach of faith and was inclined to attack [[Yüan Shu|p|ord]] himself. However, his adviser opposed this course, saying, “You should not; he is in possession of [[Shouchʻun|l|ord]] and has a large army, well supplied. You are no match for him. Rather ask [[Liu Pei|p|ord]] to take up his quarters at [[Hsiaopʻei|l|ord]] as one of your wings and, when the time comes, let him lead the attack. Then both the [[Yüans|o|ord]] will fall before you and you will be very powerful.”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_zh06', background='zh', work='San Kuo, or Romance of the Three Kingdoms (Brewitt-Taylor translation, 1925)', gutenberg_id=77416,
         marked='He said, “This seal has a history. In olden days one [[Pʻien Ho|p|ord]] saw a phœnix sitting on a certain stone at the foot of [[Ching Hill|l|ord]]. He offered the stone at court. [[King Wên|p|tit]] of [[Chʻu|l|ord]] split open the stone and found a piece of jade. Early in the [[Tsʻin|l|ord]] dynasty a jade cutter made a seal from it and [[Li Ssŭ|p|ord]] engraved the characters. Two years later it was thrown overboard in a terrific storm on the [[Tungtʻing Lake|l|ord]] as a propitiatory offering, and the storm immediately ceased. Ten years later again, when the [[Emperor|t]] was making a progress and had reached [[Huaying|l|ord]], an old man by the road side handed a seal to one of the attendants saying, “This is now restored to the ancestral dragon,” and had then disappeared. Thus the jewel returned to [[Tsʻin|l|ord]]. The next year [[Emperor Shê|p|tit]] died.”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ja01', background='ja', work='The Tale of Genji (Waley translation, 1925-1927)', gutenberg_id=66057,
         marked='At this time some Koreans came to Court and among them a fortune-teller. Hearing this, the [[Emperor|t|ord]] did not send for them to come to the Palace, because of the law against the admission of foreigners which was made by the [[Emperor Uda|p|tit]]. But in strict secrecy he sent the [[Prince|t|ord]] to the Strangers’ quarters. He went under the escort of the [[Secretary of the Right|t|ord]], who was to introduce him as his own son. The fortune teller was astonished by the boy’s lineaments and expressed his surprise by continually nodding his head: ‘He has the marks of one who might become a Father of the State, and if this were his fate, he would not stop short at any lesser degree than that of Mighty King and Emperor of all the land. But when I look again—I see that confusion and sorrow would attend his reign. But should he become a great Officer of State and Counsellor of the Realm I see no happy issue, for he would be defying those kingly signs of which I spoke before.’'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ja02', background='ja', work='The Tale of Genji (Waley translation, 1925-1927)', gutenberg_id=66057,
         marked='Next day she was in attendance upon the [[Emperor|t|ord]] and while she was waiting with other gentlewomen in the ladies’ common-room [[Genji|p|ord]] came up saying: ‘Here you are. The answer to yesterday’s letter. I am afraid it is rather far-fetched,’ and he flung a note to her. The curiosity of the other gentlewomen was violently aroused. [[Genji|p|ord]] left the room humming ‘The Lady of Mikasa Hill,’ which naturally amused [[Myōbu|p|ord]] very much. The other ladies wanted to know why the [[prince|t|ali]] was laughing to himself. Was there some joke...? ‘Oh, no,’ said [[Myōbu|p|ord]]; ‘I think it was only that he had noticed someone whose nose was a little red with the morning cold. The song he hummed was surely very appropriate.’ ‘I think it was very silly,’ said one of the ladies. ‘There is no one here to-day with a red nose. He must be thinking of [[Lady Sakon|p|tit]] or [[Higo no Uneme|p|ord]].’ They were completely mystified. When [[Myōbu|p|ord]] presented [[Genji|p|ord]]’s reply, the ladies of the [[Hitachi Palace|l|ord]] gathered round her to admire it. It was written negligently on plain white paper but was none the less very elegant. ‘Does your gift of a garment mean that you wish a greater distance than ever to be kept between us?’'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ja03', background='ja', work='The Tale of Genji (Waley translation, 1925-1927)', gutenberg_id=66057,
         marked='It was now growing dark. Someone said that the position of the Earth Star would make it unlucky for the [[Prince|t|ord]] to go back to the Palace that night; and another: ‘You are right. It is now set dead against him.’ ‘But my own palace is in the same direction!’ cried [[Genji|p|ord]]. ‘How vexing! where then shall I go?’ and promptly fell asleep. The ladies-in-waiting however, agreed that it was a very serious matter and began discussing what could be done. ‘There is [[Ki no Kami|p|ord]]’s house’ said one. This [[Ki no Kami|p|ord]] was one of [[Genji|p|ord]]’s gentlemen in waiting. ‘It is in the [[Middle River|l|ord]]’ she went on; ‘and delightfully cool and shady, for they have lately dammed the river and made it flow right through the garden.’ ‘That sounds very pleasant’ said [[Genji|p|ord]], waking up, ‘besides they are the sort of people who would not mind one’s driving right in at the front gate, if one had a mind to.’'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ja04', background='ja', work='The Tale of Genji (Waley translation, 1925-1927)', gutenberg_id=66057,
         marked='It was of the young prince that he spoke in symbol; but she did not read the letter to the end. At last the mother said ‘Though I know that long life means only bitterness, I have stayed so long in the world that even before the Pine Tree of [[Takasago|l|ord]] I should hide my head in shame. How then should I find courage to go hither and thither in the great [[Palace of a Hundred Towers|l|ord]]? Though the august summons should call me time and again, myself I could not obey. But the young prince (whether he may have heard the august wish I know not) is impatient to return, and, what is small wonder, seems very downcast in this place. Tell [[his Majesty|t|ali]] this, and whatever else of my thoughts you have here learnt from me. For a little child this house is indeed a sorry place....’ ‘They say that the child is asleep’ the quiver-bearer’s daughter answered. ‘I should like to have seen him and told the [[Emperor|t|ord]] how he looks; but I am awaited at the Palace and it must be late.’'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ja05', background='ja', work='The Tale of Genji (Waley translation, 1925-1927)', gutenberg_id=67111,
         marked='It happened that [[Fujitsubo|p|ord]] was paying one of her periodical visits to the Court, and having given a casual inspection to the exhibits of both parties she decided to suspend her usual religious observances and devote herself to a thorough study of all these works, for painting was a matter in which she had always taken a deep interest. Hearing the animated discussions which were taking place between the supporters of modern and ancient art, she suggested that those present should be formed into two teams. On [[Lady Akikonomu|p|tit]]’s side the principal names were [[Heinaishi no Suke|p|ord]], [[Jijū no Naishi|p|ord]], [[Shōshō no Myōbu|p|ord]]; on [[Lady Chūjō|p|tit]]’s,—[[Daini no Naishi no Suke|p|ord]], [[Chūjō no Myōbu|p|ord]] and [[Hyōye no Myōbu|p|ord]]. These were considered the cleverest women of the day, and [[Fujitsubo|p|ord]] promised herself very good entertainment from such an interchange of wit and knowledge as their rivalry was likely to afford.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ja06', background='ja', work='The Tale of Genji (Waley translation, 1925-1927)', gutenberg_id=67111,
         marked='Not infrequently something would happen to remind him of the [[Lady Oborozuki|p|tit]] and despite all that had happened a fresh wave of longing would beset him. She for her part had not only suffered but learnt her lesson and utterly refused to have any dealings with him, which made him feel very irritated and depressed. Now that the [[ex-Emperor Suzaku|p|tit]] was relieved of the cares of Government, he became somewhat more animated and showed a certain amount of interest in music and other Court diversions. It was curious that among all his Ladies-in-Waiting and Ladies-of-the-Wardrobe it was to [[Lady Jōkyōden|p|tit]], the mother of the [[Crown Prince|t|ord]], that he paid the least attention. Not even the singular chance which made her mother of the [[Heir Apparent|t|ali]] seemed able to restore to her any particle of the ascendancy which she had lost when [[Lady Oborozuki|p|tit]] was taken into favour. She had indeed left the [[Emperor|t|sh]]’s Palace and now lived in apartments attached to those of the [[Crown Prince|t|ord]], her son. [[Genji|p|ord]]’s rooms at Court were in the old [[Shigeisa|l|ord]]; the [[Crown Prince|t|ord]] was occupying the [[Nashitsubo|l|ord]], which was not far away. Thus [[Genji|p|ord]], as a near neighbour, was constantly consulted by the [[Prince|t|ali]]’s staff and was often able to be of considerable assistance to them.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_in01', background='in', work='The Mahabharata of Krishna-Dwaipayana Vyasa (Ganguli/Roy translation)', gutenberg_id=15474,
         marked='“[[Sauti|p|si]] replied, ‘I will recite the names of those wise ones who became the monarch’s Ritwiks and Sadasyas. The Brahmana [[Chandabhargava|p|ord]] became the Hotri in that sacrifice. He was of great reputation, and was born in the race of [[Chyavana|p|ord]] and was the foremost of those acquainted with the Vedas. The learned old Brahmana, [[Kautsa|p|ord]], became the Udgatri, the chanter of the Vedic hymns. [[Jaimini|p|si]] became the Brahmana, and [[Sarngarva|p|ord]] and [[Pingala|p|ord]] the Adhvaryus, [[Vyasa|p|ord]] with his son and disciples, and [[Uddalaka|p|ord]], [[Pramataka|p|ord]], [[Swetaketu|p|ord]], [[Pingala|p|ord]], [[Asita|p|ord]], [[Devala|p|ord]], [[Narada|p|ord]], [[Parvata|p|ord]], [[Atreya|p|ord]], [[Kundajathara|p|ord]], the Brahmana [[Kalaghata|p|ord]], [[Vatsya|p|ord]], old [[Srutasravas|p|ord]] ever engaged in japa and the study of the Vedas. [[Kohala|p|ord]] [[Devasarman|p|ord]], [[Maudgalya|p|ord]], [[Samasaurava|p|ord]], and many other Brahmanas who had got through the Vedas became the Sadasyas at that sacrifice of the son of [[Parikshit|p|ord]].'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_in02', background='in', work='The Mahabharata of Krishna-Dwaipayana Vyasa (Ganguli/Roy translation)', gutenberg_id=15474,
         marked='"[[Vaisampayana|p|si]] continued, \'Then [[Vidura|p|ord]], at the command of [[Dhritarashtra|p|ord]], repaired, O [[Bharata|p|ord]], unto [[Yajnasena|p|ali]] and the [[Pandavas|o|ord]]. And he repaired thither carrying with him numerous jewels and various kinds of wealth for [[Draupadi|p|ord]] and the [[Pandavas|o|ord]] and [[Yajnasena|p|ali]] also. Arrived at [[Drupada|p|ord]]\'s abode, [[Vidura|p|ord]] conversant with every rule of morality and deep in every science, properly accosted the monarch and waited upon him. [[Drupada|p|ord]] received [[Vidura|p|ord]] in proper form and they both enquired after each other\'s welfare. [[Vidura|p|ord]] then saw there the [[Pandavas|o|ord]] and [[Vasudeva|p|ord]]. As soon as he saw them he embraced them from affection and enquired after their well being. The [[Pandavas|o|ord]] also along with [[Vasudeva|p|ord]], in due order, worshipped [[Vidura|p|ord]] of immeasurable intelligence. But [[Vidura|p|ord]], O king, in the name of [[Dhritarashtra|p|ord]] repeatedly enquired with great affection after their welfare. He then gave, O monarch, unto the [[Pandavas|o|ord]] and [[Kunti|p|ord]] and [[Draupadi|p|ord]], and unto [[Drupada|p|ord]] and [[Drupada|p|ord]]\'s sons, the gems and various kinds of wealth that the [[Kauravas|o|ord]] had sent through him. Possessed of immeasurable intelligence, the modest [[Vidura|p|ord]] then, in the presence of the [[Pandavas|o|ord]] and [[Keshava|p|ali]], addressed the well-behaved [[Drupada|p|ord]] thus:'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_in03', background='in', work='The Mahabharata of Krishna-Dwaipayana Vyasa (Ganguli/Roy translation)', gutenberg_id=15474,
         marked='“[[Vaisampayana|p|si]] continued, ‘Then [[Yayati|p|ord]], the son of [[Nahusha|p|ord]], received back his decrepitude. And his son [[Puru|p|ord]] received back his own youth. And [[Yayati|p|ord]] was desirous of installing [[Puru|p|ord]], his youngest son, on the throne. But the four orders, with the Brahmanas at their head, then addressed the monarch thus, ‘O king, how shall thou bestow thy kingdom on [[Puru|p|ord]], passing over thy eldest son [[Yadu|p|ord]] born of [[Devayani|p|ord]], and, therefore, the grandson of the great [[Sukra|p|ord]]? Indeed, [[Yadu|p|ord]] is thy eldest son; after him hath been born [[Turvasu|p|ord]]; and of [[Sarmishtha|p|ord]]’s sons, the first is [[Drahyu|p|ord]], then [[Anu|p|ord]] and then [[Puru|p|ord]]. How doth the youngest deserve the throne, passing all his elder brothers over? This we represent to thee! O, conform to virtuous practice.’'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_in04', background='in', work='The Mahabharata of Krishna-Dwaipayana Vyasa (Ganguli/Roy translation)', gutenberg_id=15475,
         marked='“[[Yudhishthira|p|si]] replied, ‘Ye sons of the [[Kuru|o|ord]] race, ye bulls among men, hear what I shall do on appearing before [[king Virata|p|tit]]. Presenting myself as a Brahmana, [[Kanka|p|ord]] by name, skilled in dice and fond of play, I shall become a courtier of that high-souled king. And moving upon chess-boards beautiful pawns made of ivory, of blue and yellow and red and white hue, by throws of black and red dice, I shall entertain the king with his courtiers and friends. And while I shall continue to thus delight the king, nobody will succeed in discovering me. And should the monarch ask me, I shall say, “Formerly I was the bosom friend of [[Yudhishthira|p|ord]].” I tell you that it is thus that I shall pass my days (in the city of [[Virata|l|ord]]). What office wilt thou, O [[Vrikodara|p|ali]], fill in the city of [[Virata|l|ord]]?’”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_in05', background='in', work='The Mahabharata of Krishna-Dwaipayana Vyasa (Ganguli/Roy translation)', gutenberg_id=15475,
         marked='“[[Arjuna|p|si]] replied, ‘O lord of the Earth, I will declare myself as one of the neuter sex. O monarch, it is, indeed difficult to hide the marks of the bowstring on my arms. I will, however, cover both my cicatrized arms with bangles. Wearing brilliant rings on my ears and conch-bangles on my wrists and causing a braid to hang down from my head, I shall, O king, appear as one of the third sex, [[Vrihannala|p|ali]] by name. And living as a female I shall (always) entertain the king and the inmates of the inner apartments by reciting stories. And, O king, I shall also instruct the women of [[Virata|p|ord]]’s palace in singing and delightful modes of dancing and in musical instruments of diverse kinds. And I shall also recite the various excellent acts of men and thus conceal myself, O son of [[Kunti|p|ord]], by feigning disguise. And, O [[Bharata|p|ord]] should the king enquire, I will say that, I lived as a waiting maid of [[Draupadi|p|ord]] in [[Yudhishthira|p|ord]]’s palace. And, O foremost of kings, concealing myself by this means, as fire is concealed by ashes, I shall pass my days agreeably in the palace of [[Virata|p|ord]].’”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_in06', background='in', work='The Mahabharata of Krishna-Dwaipayana Vyasa (Ganguli/Roy translation)', gutenberg_id=15475,
         marked='[[Vaisampayana|p|si]] continued, “Then [[Draupadi|p|ord]], accompanied by those girls entered the royal abode, desirous of appearing before [[Sudeshna|p|ord]]. And when she came before the queen, [[Virata|p|ord]]’s wife addressed her at the command of the king, saying, ‘Do thou, O [[Sairindhri|p|ali]], speedily go whithersoever thou likest. The king, good betide thee, hath been filled with fear at this discomfiture at the hands of the [[Gandharvas|o|ord]]. Thou art, O thou of graceful eye-brows, young and unparalleled on earth in beauty. Thou art, besides, an object of desire with men. The [[Gandharvas|o|ord]] again, are exceedingly wrathful.’ Thereat [[Sairindhri|p|ali]] said, ‘O beauteous lady, let the king suffer me to live here for only thirteen days more. Without doubt, the [[Gandharvas|o|ord]] also will be highly obliged at this. They will then convey me hence and do what would be agreeable to [[Virata|p|ord]]. Without doubt, the king, by doing this, with his friends, will reap great benefit.’”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ko01', background='ko', work='Korean Fairy Tales (Griffis, 1922)', gutenberg_id=67180,
         marked='Over the great plains of [[Manchuria|l|ord]], [[Kija|p|ord]] and his army of people, little folks and big ones, marched ever toward the rising sun, until they crossed the [[Duck Green River|l|ord]], which we call the [[Yalu|l|ali]]. After a few days more, they came to the [[Great Eastern River|l|ord]] ([[Ta Tong|l|ali]]). There the land was very beautiful and [[Kija|p|ord]] resolved to settle and build a city. From the tinted clouds at sunrise, rosy, golden, flushed with every shade of red, and lovely with changing colors the new country had been named [[Cho-sen|l|ord]], or [[Land of Morning Radiance|l|ali]]. As the sun rose and raced toward the west, where his homeland lay, [[Kija|p|ord]] welcomed the good omen as a double blessing. He saw in the calm of his first day in his adopted country a threefold pledge of continued good-will between the new kingdom and the old empire, Heaven’s favoring sign of his loyalty to the [[Chinese Emperor|t|ord]], and the surety of good-will from the spirit of the [[Ever White Mountain|l|ord]].'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ko02', background='ko', work='Korean Fairy Tales (Griffis, 1922)', gutenberg_id=67180,
         marked='The old couple seemed able to eat and enjoy twice as big dinners as formerly, for all the while the adopted [[Bullfrog|p|wl]] was very entertaining. [[Mr. and Mrs. Pak|p|tit]] laughed continually, declaring they had never heard such good stories as he told. The good wife was, however, quite equal to her guest in retailing gossip. One of her favorite subjects, of which she never tired, was the beauty and charm of [[Miss Peach|p|tit]]. She was the accomplished daughter of the big Yang-ban, or nobleman, [[Mr. Poom|p|tit]], who lived in a great house, with a host of servants and retainers in the next village, and [[Mrs. Pak|p|tit]] insisted there was no young woman in the world like her. It was noticed that [[Mr. Bullfrog|p|tit]] was particularly interested when [[Miss Peach Poom|p|tit]] was the subject of the old lady’s praises.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ko03', background='ko', work='Korean Fairy Tales (Griffis, 1922)', gutenberg_id=67180,
         marked='One after the other they were presented to [[King Peony|p|tit]], and gracefully made their salute. But of them all, the [[king|t|ali]] seemed most to favor [[Miss Cinnamon Flower|p|tit]]. He let the others pass out from the Court, but lingered long with the spicy visitor, spending much time in her society, as if smitten with her charms. By and by he invited [[Miss Cinnamon Rose|p|tit]] to come and live in the palace, and leaving his ministers to carry on the government, he spent all his time in her society. She was installed in a place near [[His Majesty|t|ali]] and seemed always to have his ear and attention, even when the [[king|t|ali]]’s prime minister had to wait long for an audience, or even a word. [[Miss Cinnamon Rose|p|tit]] seemed to be the real ruler instead of the [[king|t|ali]] himself.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ko04', background='ko', work='Korean Fairy Tales (Griffis, 1922)', gutenberg_id=67180,
         marked='What a clatter of gossip was at once heard in both villages! No one ever thought that such a handsome fellow as [[Mr. Long Body Wum|p|tit]] would ever marry into the [[Pil family|o|ord]]. Some jealous folks hinted that [[Mr. Long Body|p|tit]], if he took a wife with a thousand feet, would never be able to pay his shoemaker. On the other hand, so long as his bride would be content with plain twine shoes all might go well; but for extra occasions, or if his wife were extravagant and wanted lady’s turned up house foot-gear made of red morocco such as only the rich folks wear,—well there would be trouble in the household. How could he keep her in shoes? Other persons, however, who knew that the [[Pils|o|ord]] were famous people, wondered how [[Mr. Wum|p|tit]] ever managed to get such a prize as [[Miss Pil|p|tit]].'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ko05', background='ko', work='Korean Fairy Tales (Griffis, 1922)', gutenberg_id=67180,
         marked='Twelve centuries ago lived the renowned scholar [[Sul Chong|p|ord]], the greatest of all the learned men of [[Korea|l|ord]]. His head was as full of knowledge as a persimmon is of pulp, and his ideas were as numerous as the seeds in a pomegranate. He taught his countrymen all that was in the books of [[China|l|ord]], and in the temple of [[Confucius|p|ord]] his portrait hangs to this day. He lived in the kingdom of [[Silla|l|ord]], in the days of its glory, when ships from [[Japan|l|ord]] and [[China|l|ord]] sailed into its seaports and the Arabs from [[Bagdad|l|ord]] brought their pretty wares to exchange for gold, ginseng, camphor, porcelain, cinnamon, ginger and tiger skins, to take to their renowned [[Caliph|t|ord]] and his turbaned nobles at court, of whom we read in the “Arabian Nights.”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_ko06', background='ko', work='Korean Fairy Tales (Griffis, 1922)', gutenberg_id=67180,
         marked='“Long, long ago, before there were any refined people in the [[Land of Dawn|l|ord]], and no men but rude savages, a bear and a tiger met together. It was on the southern slope of [[Old Whitehead Mountain|l|ord]] in the forest. These wild animals were not satisfied with the kind of human beings already on the earth, and they wanted better ones. They thought that if they could become human they would be able to improve upon the quality. So these patriotic beasts, the bear and the tiger, agreed to go before [[Hananim|p|ord]], the [[Great One of Heaven and Earth|t|ali]], and ask him to change at once their form and nature; or, at least, tell them how it could be done.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_eu01', background='eu', work='Pride and Prejudice (Austen)', gutenberg_id=1342,
         marked='[[Lady Catherine|p|si]] seemed resigned. “[[Mrs. Collins|p|si]], you must send a servant with them. You know I always speak my mind, and I cannot bear the idea of two young women travelling post by themselves. It is highly improper. You must contrive to send somebody. I have the greatest dislike in the world to that sort of thing. Young women should always be properly guarded and attended, according to their situation in life. When my niece [[Georgiana|p|ord]] went to [[Ramsgate|l|ord]] last summer, I made a point of her having two men-servants go with her. [[Miss Darcy|p|tit]], the daughter of [[Mr. Darcy|p|tit]] of [[Pemberley|l|ord]], and [[Lady Anne|p|tit]], could not have appeared with propriety in a different manner. I am excessively attentive to all those things. You must send [[John|p|ord]] with the young ladies, [[Mrs. Collins|p|tit]]. I am glad it occurred to me to mention it; for it would really be discreditable to you to let them go alone.”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_eu02', background='eu', work='Pride and Prejudice (Austen)', gutenberg_id=1342,
         marked='When the gentlemen had joined them, and tea was over, the card tables were placed. [[Lady Catherine|p|tit]], [[Sir William|p|tit]], and [[Mr. and Mrs. Collins|p|tit]] sat down to quadrille; and as [[Miss De Bourgh|p|tit]] chose to play at cassino, the two girls had the honour of assisting [[Mrs. Jenkinson|p|tit]] to make up her party. Their table was superlatively stupid. Scarcely a syllable was uttered that did not relate to the game, except when [[Mrs. Jenkinson|p|tit]] expressed her fears of [[Miss De Bourgh|p|tit]]’s being too hot or too cold, or having too much or too little light. A great deal more passed at the other table. [[Lady Catherine|p|si]] was generally speaking--stating the mistakes of the three others, or relating some anecdote of herself. [[Mr. Collins|p|si]] was employed in agreeing to everything [[her Ladyship|t|ali]] said, thanking her for every fish he won, and apologizing if he thought he won too many. [[Sir William|p|si]] did not say much. He was storing his memory with anecdotes and noble names.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_eu03', background='eu', work='Pride and Prejudice (Austen)', gutenberg_id=1342,
         marked='“Well, girls,” said she, as soon as they were left to themselves, “what say you to the day? I think everything has passed off uncommonly well, I assure you. The dinner was as well dressed as any I ever saw. The venison was roasted to a turn--and everybody said, they never saw so fat a haunch. The soup was fifty times better than what we had at the [[Lucases|o|ord]]’ last week; and even [[Mr. Darcy|p|tit]] acknowledged that the partridges were remarkably well done; and I suppose he has two or three French cooks at least. And, my dear [[Jane|p|ord]], I never saw you look in greater beauty. [[Mrs. Long|p|tit]] said so too, for I asked her whether you did not. And what do you think she said besides? ‘Ah! [[Mrs. Bennet|p|tit]], we shall have her at [[Netherfield|l|ord]] at last!’ She did, indeed. I do think [[Mrs. Long|p|tit]] is as good a creature as ever lived--and her nieces are very pretty behaved girls, and not at all handsome: I like them prodigiously.”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_eu04', background='eu', work='Pride and Prejudice (Austen)', gutenberg_id=1342,
         marked='It is not the object of this work to give a description of [[Derbyshire|l|ord]], nor of any of the remarkable places through which their route thither lay--[[Oxford|l|ord]], [[Blenheim|l|ord]], [[Warwick|l|ord]], [[Kenilworth|l|ord]], [[Birmingham|l|ord]], etc., are sufficiently known. A small part of [[Derbyshire|l|ord]] is all the present concern. To the little town of [[Lambton|l|ord]], the scene of [[Mrs. Gardiner|p|tit]]’s former residence, and where she had lately learned that some acquaintance still remained, they bent their steps, after having seen all the principal wonders of the country; and within five miles of [[Lambton|l|ord]], [[Elizabeth|p|ord]] found, from her aunt, that [[Pemberley|l|ord]] was situated. It was not in their direct road; nor more than a mile or two out of it. In talking over their route the evening before, [[Mrs. Gardiner|p|tit]] expressed an inclination to see the place again. [[Mr. Gardiner|p|tit]] declared his willingness, and [[Elizabeth|p|ord]] was applied to for her approbation.'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_eu05', background='eu', work='Kalevala, the Epic Poem of Finland (Crawford translation, 1888)', gutenberg_id=5186,
         marked='[[Louhi|p|si]], hostess of [[Pohyola|l|ord]], [[Northland|l|ord]]’s old and toothless wizard, Makes the [[Sun|p|wl]] and [[Moon|p|wl]] her captives; In her arms she takes fair [[Luna|p|ali]] From her cradle in the birch-tree, Calls the [[Sun|p|wl]] down from his station, From the fir-tree’s bending branches, Carries them to upper [[Northland|l|ord]], To the darksome [[Sariola|l|ord]]; Hides the [[Moon|p|wl]], no more to glimmer, In a rock of many colors; Hides the [[Sun|p|wl]], to shine no longer, In the iron-banded mountain; Thereupon these words she utters: “[[Moon|p|wl]] of gold and [[Sun|p|wl]] of silver, Hide your faces in the caverns Of [[Pohyola|l|ord]]’s dismal mountain; Shine no more to gladden [[Northland|l|ord]], Till I come to give ye freedom, Drawn by coursers nine in number, Sable coursers of one mother!”'),
    dict(doc_id='pd_eu06', background='eu', work='Kalevala, the Epic Poem of Finland (Crawford translation, 1888)', gutenberg_id=5186,
         marked='Little caring, [[Lemminkainen|p|ord]] Entered service there as shepherd, In the daytime on the pastures, In the evening, making merry At the games of lively maidens, At the dances with the virgins, With the maids with braided tresses. Thus it was that [[Lemminkainen|p|ord]], Thus the shepherd, [[Kaukomieli|p|ali]], Quickly hushed the women’s laughter, Quickly quenched the taunts of maidens, Quickly silenced their derision. All the dames and [[Sahri|l|ord]]-daughters Soon were feasting [[Lemminkainen|p|ord]], At his side they danced and lingered. Only was there one among them, One among the [[Sahri|l|ord]]-virgins, Harbored neither love nor wooers, Favored neither gods nor heroes, This the lovely maid [[Kyllikki|p|ord]], This the [[Sahri|l|ord]]’s fairest flower. [[Lemminkainen|p|ord]], full of pleasure, Handsome hero, [[Kaukomieli|p|ali]], Rowed a hundred boats in pieces, Pulled a thousand oars to fragments, While he wooed the [[Maid of Beauty|t|ali]], Tried to win the fair [[Kyllikki|p|ord]].'),
]
